And Rain, as Dwight, keeps kicking the car.

1044
00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:10.000
Yeah.

1045
00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:11.600
And Phyllis –

1046
00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:12.420
She didn't care for it.

1047
00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:18.300
She was very nervous about it, and I guess at some point, she went up to Rain and said,

1048
00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:23.280
Listen, it's totally fine if you kick my car, but don't kick the car or the hubcap.

1049
00:39:23.500 --> 00:39:24.200
Just the tire.

1050
00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:24.940
Just the tire.

1051
00:39:25.540 --> 00:39:28.280
We used to park our cars in that little parking lot.

1052
00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:28.600
Yeah.

1053
00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:29.380
And then –

1054
00:39:29.380 --> 00:39:33.500
A lot of our real cars have appeared as cars in the show.

1055
00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:36.640
And then they – in seasons later, they had us move our cars.

1056
00:39:36.640 --> 00:39:39.720
I think maybe they realized there's, like, liability or something.

1057
00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:43.820
I mean, so – but in those early days, those were our cars.

1058
00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:55.500
A very, very big element of this episode is the point where Dwight ends up in a box in the warehouse to spy.

1059
00:39:55.920 --> 00:40:05.320
And more than any other question, when I put this out to fans for questions, the most questions that we got, the most frequently asked questions, were about this box.

1060
00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:05.720
Right.

1061
00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:07.640
How long was Rain in the box?

1062
00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:09.400
How many takes did we do?

1063
00:40:09.860 --> 00:40:11.720
How many boxes did we use?

1064
00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:19.820
One of the questions was there's a scene where Pam is on the phone doing a fake phone call, and the box kind of tips over.

1065
00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:20.900
Wait, wait, wait, wait.

1066
00:40:20.900 --> 00:40:21.780
And I laugh and run off.

1067
00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:22.860
Wait, I have a note card.

1068
00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:24.260
I have a note card about this.

1069
00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:24.720
What is it?

1070
00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:29.500
I want to – okay, I know you put it out to Twitter, but this is your BFF, so I get to –

1071
00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:30.280
Can I ask you first?

1072
00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:30.800
You get the first question.

1073
00:40:31.260 --> 00:40:31.920
Jenna, question.

1074
00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:32.620
Semi-colon.

1075
00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:35.360
Did you really break and laugh by the box?

1076
00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:39.040
I think you did because I know what happens to your shoulders when you really laugh.

1077
00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:40.320
This was the question.

1078
00:40:40.540 --> 00:40:40.760
Ah!

1079
00:40:41.080 --> 00:40:43.040
This is the question that people want to know.

1080
00:40:43.960 --> 00:40:45.420
I think I faked it.

1081
00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:46.080
What?

1082
00:40:46.360 --> 00:40:47.840
Yeah, I don't think I really broke.

1083
00:40:48.040 --> 00:40:49.360
I don't want to believe that.

1084
00:40:49.360 --> 00:40:49.880
I know.

1085
00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:55.840
I think – because I remember shooting that scene, and I remember it was very technical.

1086
00:40:56.140 --> 00:40:56.380
Okay.

1087
00:40:56.520 --> 00:41:00.940
So it was – I – it was happening over my shoulder.

1088
00:41:01.240 --> 00:41:08.860
Stuff was happening over my shoulder, but there was a way where they wanted me to break at a certain moment, so it was kind of technical.

1089
00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:11.640
So you had to sort of get out of the way, and the box was tipping.

1090
00:41:11.640 --> 00:41:12.380
Yes, exactly.

1091
00:41:12.620 --> 00:41:18.880
So I remember shooting it a number of times, and I remember thinking to myself, gosh, does this even look real?

1092
00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:24.020
Like, do I – it's a really – there are two things that I think are really hard to do when you're acting.

1093
00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:25.160
One of them is cry.

1094
00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:25.720
Right.

1095
00:41:25.840 --> 00:41:27.120
But I think people would expect that.

1096
00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:29.380
But the other is to laugh convincingly.

1097
00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:29.680
Yes.

1098
00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:32.620
You know who has the best fake laugh is Steve Carell.

1099
00:41:32.980 --> 00:41:37.560
Steve Carell can do this fake laugh that is the most honest, genuine laugh.

1100
00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:43.140
And every time he would do it, I would start to laugh, even though I knew it was a fake laugh.

1101
00:41:43.320 --> 00:41:44.500
It was so contagious.

1102
00:41:44.820 --> 00:41:46.260
He is the best at it.

1103
00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:51.660
I find it's easier to cry than to fake laugh myself for acting.

1104
00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:52.340
Fake laughing is very difficult.

1105
00:41:52.680 --> 00:41:53.080
It is.

1106
00:41:53.160 --> 00:42:00.840
The reason I think that I didn't really break is because I remember having anxiety over whether or not anyone was going to buy it.

1107
00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:03.620
Jenna, I have a little something on my note card here.

1108
00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:04.260
What is it?

1109
00:42:04.280 --> 00:42:05.220
I have another note card.

1110
00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:08.600
I wrote, this is the first time we see Daryl.

1111
00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:09.180
Yes.

1112
00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:10.120
Yes.

1113
00:42:10.300 --> 00:42:14.840
When rain pops out of the box at the end, Daryl is down there.

1114
00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:15.440
Yes.

1115
00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:17.400
Now, he is not going to have a big scene.

1116
00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:20.680
We're not going to hear him speak until the next episode, which is basketball.

1117
00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:21.320
Yes.

1118
00:42:21.500 --> 00:42:24.040
But that is the first time we see Daryl, so I was very excited.

1119
00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:24.620
I know.

1120
00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:26.540
And look, my note card came in handy.

1121
00:42:26.920 --> 00:42:27.980
I love that about her.

1122
00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:31.340
I love how we plant seeds that grow later.

1123
00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:31.820
Right.

1124
00:42:32.900 --> 00:42:41.560
That is a person who is going to be a major character on the show, but we introduce him just as a guy seeing rain come out of the box.

1125
00:42:41.560 --> 00:42:47.940
Yeah, that is one of the things, and I know we say it over and over, and we will for the life of this podcaSt.

1126
00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:52.880
But one of the things I love about what Greg Daniels did is he starts in the middle.

1127
00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:55.420
He doesn't like spoon feed you information.

1128
00:42:55.880 --> 00:42:59.220
Just the way they design the show, you play catch up as an audience member.

1129
00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:01.700
You're just like a day in the life, and you're right in the middle.

1130
00:43:01.860 --> 00:43:01.980
Yes.

1131
00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:02.860
And I love that.

1132
00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:03.320
Yes.

1133
00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:04.100
I love that, too.

1134
00:43:04.420 --> 00:43:04.720
Okay.

1135
00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:07.200
Here's a big confession about the box.

1136
00:43:07.440 --> 00:43:08.780
This is going to blow your mind.

1137
00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:09.220
What?

1138
00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:13.780
Rain wasn't in the box most of the time.

1139
00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:15.260
Who was in the box?

1140
00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:16.540
Who was in the box?

1141
00:43:16.620 --> 00:43:17.220
Who was in the box?

1142
00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:19.440
Phil Shea, our props.

1143
00:43:19.460 --> 00:43:20.100
Prop guy.

1144
00:43:20.260 --> 00:43:20.420
Yeah.

1145
00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:21.360
Phil Shea.

1146
00:43:21.440 --> 00:43:21.640
Yeah.

1147
00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:23.260
Phil Shea is a very tall person.

1148
00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:24.360
Yeah, well, so is Rain.

1149
00:43:24.620 --> 00:43:26.500
And he was in that box all the time.

1150
00:43:26.620 --> 00:43:32.420
So most of the box acting was done by Phil Shea, of course, except for the end where Rain busts out.

1151
00:43:32.420 --> 00:43:37.440
So in that scene where I'm cracking up, Phil Shea was in the box.

1152
00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:38.160
Oh, my gosh.

1153
00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:39.400
You know what I think we should do, Angela?

1154
00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:40.300
Tell me, Jenna.

1155
00:43:40.520 --> 00:43:45.900
I think we should call Phil Shea and get him to answer all of our questions about the box.

1156
00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:47.160
What was it like in the box?

1157
00:43:47.380 --> 00:43:49.400
Phil Shea, we are about to blow up your phone.

1158
00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:53.030
Phil?

1159
00:43:53.370 --> 00:43:53.730
Yes.

1160
00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:55.510
Phil Shea, it's Jenna and Angela.

1161
00:43:55.730 --> 00:43:56.370
Hi, Phil.

1162
00:43:57.370 --> 00:43:58.130
Oh, hey, guys.

1163
00:43:58.190 --> 00:43:58.590
How are you?

1164
00:43:58.790 --> 00:43:58.990
Good.

1165
00:43:58.990 --> 00:43:59.730
How are you?

1166
00:44:00.070 --> 00:44:02.670
Thank you so much for letting us call and bug you.

1167
00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:06.270
It's always a pleasure to help out Pam and Angela.

1168
00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:07.710
Oh, my gosh.

1169
00:44:08.090 --> 00:44:09.210
Oh, Phil, thank you so much.

1170
00:44:09.250 --> 00:44:11.250
It's just so nice to hear your voice, I have to say.

1171
00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:12.110
That's what I was thinking.

1172
00:44:12.350 --> 00:44:14.230
I was like, gosh, it's so nice to hear your voice.

1173
00:44:15.090 --> 00:44:17.450
Hey, what an experience we had for nine years, right?

1174
00:44:17.910 --> 00:44:18.270
Yes.

1175
00:44:18.570 --> 00:44:19.670
We were the luckieSt.

1176
00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:20.710
We were the luckieSt.

1177
00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:25.590
Well, if you have a second, Phil, can we ask you some questions from the Alliance episode?

1178
00:44:26.750 --> 00:44:27.110
Absolutely.

1179
00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:36.970
So we opened up questions to the fans, and overwhelmingly, the thing people wanted to know about was the box that Rain gets into in the warehouse.

1180
00:44:38.110 --> 00:44:39.150
Oh, my gosh.

1181
00:44:39.370 --> 00:44:43.370
So they wanted to know how long he was in the box, everything about the box.

1182
00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:45.950
But, Phil, I know you were in the box.

1183
00:44:46.190 --> 00:44:52.590
Rain only got in after all the shooing and the scooching and the sliding and then the tumbling was done.

1184
00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:55.030
Phil, you did all of that in the box?

1185
00:44:55.030 --> 00:44:57.570
You were the scoocher and the tumbler and all that?

1186
00:44:58.250 --> 00:44:59.050
I was.

1187
00:44:59.390 --> 00:45:04.510
Apparently, Mr. Zabornak, our line producer, didn't think we had enough money to hire a stunt guy.

1188
00:45:04.710 --> 00:45:07.230
So he assigned me to be in the box.

1189
00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:10.770
And I remember I had to go out and buy my own elbow pads and knee pads.

1190
00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:21.910
And then I had a loose walkie-talkie in there with me with Liz Ryan, who was the first AP, shouting out all of Brian Gordon, who was our director for that episode's command.

1191
00:45:22.310 --> 00:45:23.950
I remember like it was yesterday.

1192
00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:26.530
So, Phil, can you take me through it from the beginning?

1193
00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:30.810
You get a script, and it says this character has to be in a box.

1194
00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:32.310
So what do you do?

1195
00:45:32.370 --> 00:45:35.650
Because you're in charge of getting the box as the prop master.

1196
00:45:35.830 --> 00:45:37.110
So where do you start?

1197
00:45:37.110 --> 00:45:48.710
On that particular prop, I sought out Greg Daniels, and I said, do you see Rain sitting down squatting in the box, or is he kneeling down on his hands and knees?

1198
00:45:48.850 --> 00:45:50.350
And he said, kneeling on his hands and knees.

1199
00:45:50.490 --> 00:45:51.850
And Rain is tall.

1200
00:45:52.030 --> 00:45:52.990
Rain is tall, right?

1201
00:45:53.250 --> 00:45:54.330
And so are you, Phil.

1202
00:45:54.490 --> 00:45:55.190
You're very tall.

1203
00:45:55.450 --> 00:45:55.610
Yeah.

1204
00:45:56.150 --> 00:45:58.170
Yeah, Rain is, I think, 6'1 or 6'2.

1205
00:45:58.290 --> 00:46:00.190
I'm 6'1 if I blow dry my hair.

1206
00:46:01.170 --> 00:46:02.530
You do have great hair.

1207
00:46:03.250 --> 00:46:05.610
You guys should know, Phil has an amazing head of hair.

1208
00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:06.210
He really does.

1209
00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:06.970
Yes, this is true.

1210
00:46:07.310 --> 00:46:07.870
I leased it.

1211
00:46:07.950 --> 00:46:08.490
I leased it.

1212
00:46:08.590 --> 00:46:09.670
It's a Hollywood fake hairdo.

1213
00:46:09.830 --> 00:46:15.990
But I tracked Rain down, and I asked him to get on his hands and knees, and I was measuring for this box.

1214
00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:19.710
Wait, you made Rain get on his hands and knees, and then you measured him?

1215
00:46:19.890 --> 00:46:21.470
And you measured him to know how big it was?

1216
00:46:21.790 --> 00:46:22.910
That was season one.

1217
00:46:23.070 --> 00:46:24.170
Rain was willing to do anything.

1218
00:46:24.370 --> 00:46:29.410
Then I went to a couple box cities around Southern California, and no one had a box close to that.

1219
00:46:29.410 --> 00:46:34.870
But then I went on to the Internet, and I looked at box manufacturers in Southern California.

1220
00:46:35.090 --> 00:46:40.750
I found a company in San Diego named Kent Landsberg.

1221
00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:46.990
And you have to understand, as prop people, we have a very short production time and lead time to get all these props.

1222
00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:51.130
So I called this Kent Landsberg paper company and asked to speak to the manager.

1223
00:46:51.890 --> 00:46:55.670
And in a much more frenetic and hyper voice, I said, look, you've got to help me out.

1224
00:46:55.770 --> 00:46:57.310
I have to have a custom box made.

1225
00:46:57.410 --> 00:47:00.890
I probably need about 100 of them, and they need to be this dimension.

1226
00:47:01.170 --> 00:47:04.330
And San Diego is like three hours away, too.

1227
00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:06.510
Well, and also, that's such a good point.

1228
00:47:06.550 --> 00:47:08.250
You didn't need one box.

1229
00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:09.330
You needed 100.

1230
00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:12.510
You needed 100 boxes because you didn't know how many takes we were going to do.

1231
00:47:12.510 --> 00:47:18.310
And Brian Gordon, the director of this episode, ended up being notorious for doing tons of takes.

1232
00:47:18.670 --> 00:47:21.770
So you were right to cover your bases and get 100.

1233
00:47:22.170 --> 00:47:22.590
Yes.

1234
00:47:23.130 --> 00:47:23.490
Right.

1235
00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:26.070
And we shot that scene in the Dunder Mifflin warehouse.

1236
00:47:26.070 --> 00:47:28.070
We wanted to have some boxes on the shelves.

1237
00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:29.530
Oh, right.

1238
00:47:29.570 --> 00:47:31.350
Just ones that he didn't bust out of.

1239
00:47:31.970 --> 00:47:32.230
Yeah.

1240
00:47:32.390 --> 00:47:32.530
Right.

1241
00:47:32.550 --> 00:47:33.030
Of course.

1242
00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:34.650
It wasn't a prop box.

1243
00:47:34.750 --> 00:47:35.690
It just appeared out of nowhere.

1244
00:47:35.690 --> 00:47:38.910
So we had to give some legitimacy to where it originated from.

1245
00:47:39.010 --> 00:47:40.110
Look at all the detail.

1246
00:47:40.390 --> 00:47:41.310
Look at all the detail.

1247
00:47:41.310 --> 00:47:42.650
So how did you do it?

1248
00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:43.370
So how did you do it?

1249
00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:45.030
This guy said he would do it, no problem.

1250
00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:46.290
And then how did you get them?

1251
00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:48.510
Well, I talked to him.

1252
00:47:48.570 --> 00:47:50.510
He said the lead time is usually a month and a half.

1253
00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:52.030
I said, we have four days.

1254
00:47:52.070 --> 00:47:52.430
Oh, no.

1255
00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:54.250
I said, I need the boxes in four days.

1256
00:47:54.270 --> 00:47:55.590
And I said, it was for a TV show.

1257
00:47:55.710 --> 00:47:58.550
He said, it was for The Office, which is a remake of the British version.

1258
00:47:58.690 --> 00:48:03.330
And he probably said, I'm sorry, I'm a huge fan of the British version, and I'm not interested in helping you.

1259
00:48:03.330 --> 00:48:03.890
I've never heard of your show.

1260
00:48:05.590 --> 00:48:07.570
It's like you guys were listening into the phone call.

1261
00:48:07.730 --> 00:48:08.530
Exactly what he said.

1262
00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:11.650
No, no, he said he would be glad to make an exception for a TV show.

1263
00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:12.650
So he made it happen.

1264
00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:17.090
We got the boxes delivered to Culver City, which is where we shot that episode in our first season.

1265
00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:21.930
And then they put the graphics on the box, and we had them back two days before we were filming.

1266
00:48:21.930 --> 00:48:24.730
So we had it all done and ranged it in the box fine,

1267
00:48:24.730 --> 00:48:31.990
and then it was just about coordinating where the holes would be inserted by me when I'm inside the box with Dwight's Swiss Army knife.

1268
00:48:32.110 --> 00:48:33.990
And it was a great scene.

1269
00:48:34.230 --> 00:48:35.870
I mean, I remember we shot that down.

1270
00:48:35.990 --> 00:48:37.730
I think it was a Friday night we shot that scene.

1271
00:48:37.810 --> 00:48:38.590
But it was fun.

1272
00:48:38.590 --> 00:48:39.530
Everybody was laughing.

1273
00:48:39.790 --> 00:48:41.710
The crew was always laughing on that show.

1274
00:48:41.710 --> 00:48:45.530
And it was a great scene, and the fans love it so much.

1275
00:48:45.570 --> 00:48:47.450
Oh, Phil, thank you so much.

1276
00:48:47.810 --> 00:48:52.870
I'm looking at a picture that I can email you that Michael Gallenberg gave me.

1277
00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:57.790
I'm going to send you a picture as soon as we hang up of me getting ready to tape up the fist box.

1278
00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:03.910
And I don't know if you have the ability to post the picture on the podcast, but I'm going to take a picture.

1279
00:49:03.910 --> 00:49:05.310
We can put it on our website, maybe.

1280
00:49:05.670 --> 00:49:05.950
Yeah.

1281
00:49:05.950 --> 00:49:11.450
Okay, I'll take a picture, and you can see by the expression on my face after about take 50.

1282
00:49:13.030 --> 00:49:14.810
You were like in a sweat lodge.

1283
00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:17.050
Oh, Phil, thank you.

1284
00:49:17.470 --> 00:49:22.450
But, Jenna, you were there, and you remember the last take when Brian Gordon said one more.

1285
00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:25.450
I hope you remember this, where he had me do all this stuff.

1286
00:49:25.570 --> 00:49:28.850
And then when I cut myself out of the box, everybody had went and hit.

1287
00:49:29.270 --> 00:49:29.970
Oh, that's right.

1288
00:49:29.990 --> 00:49:31.930
I came out into the warehouse.

1289
00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:36.730
So he at least played a practical joke on me at the end to make me laugh, so that was pretty funny.

1290
00:49:36.750 --> 00:49:37.250
We did.

1291
00:49:37.250 --> 00:49:43.730
We left you in the box, and we pretended like we were still filming, and then you just were there for so long.

1292
00:49:43.810 --> 00:49:44.930
That is horrible, you guys.

1293
00:49:44.930 --> 00:49:46.170
That is kind of horrible.

1294
00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:47.510
Why were we so mean?

1295
00:49:47.770 --> 00:49:51.050
Phil, if I was there, Phil, I would have come up and said, Phil, get out.

1296
00:49:51.110 --> 00:49:51.590
They left.

1297
00:49:52.090 --> 00:49:53.410
It was so funny.

1298
00:49:53.570 --> 00:49:53.910
No, it was funny.

1299
00:49:53.910 --> 00:49:54.570
It was very funny.

1300
00:49:54.570 --> 00:49:55.190
It was a great scene.

1301
00:49:55.710 --> 00:50:00.090
Phil, can I throw two questions at you really quickly before we let you go?

1302
00:50:00.130 --> 00:50:00.630
Oh, okay.

1303
00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:00.910
Okay.

1304
00:50:00.910 --> 00:50:01.530
Yeah.

1305
00:50:01.950 --> 00:50:06.990
So after getting this box sounds like it was a massive undertaking.

1306
00:50:07.290 --> 00:50:09.350
Phil, your job is sounding really fun.

1307
00:50:09.490 --> 00:50:15.710
Like you read an episode, and then you have to come up with all these weird objects and gags.

1308
00:50:15.970 --> 00:50:17.890
It sounds fun and stressful, Phil.

1309
00:50:18.270 --> 00:50:21.430
Like how do you get 100 boxes in four days?

1310
00:50:22.330 --> 00:50:23.750
It's always fun.

1311
00:50:23.890 --> 00:50:33.770
It's always stressful, and it's great to be, like I said, a small part of a production that resonates with viewers and fans across generations as the office did.

1312
00:50:33.770 --> 00:50:35.910
And so it is fun.

1313
00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:42.630
It is stressful, but it's a real joy to do it when everybody laughs and everybody enjoys the prop.

1314
00:50:42.630 --> 00:50:47.590
And it adds a little value to the scripted scene, and then we all go home and laugh and had a good day.

1315
00:50:47.750 --> 00:50:47.930
Yeah.

1316
00:50:47.930 --> 00:50:48.310
So, yeah.

1317
00:50:48.550 --> 00:50:53.050
Well, we sure appreciate you, and we are going to be calling you a lot, the office lady.

1318
00:50:53.230 --> 00:50:54.110
So thank you.

1319
00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:55.570
Thanks for taking the time, Phil.

1320
00:50:56.210 --> 00:51:00.950
I have vivid memories of all the props we made for the office, so anytime you guys want to call me.

1321
00:51:01.010 --> 00:51:01.450
Can we?

1322
00:51:01.470 --> 00:51:02.010
Get behind the scenes.

1323
00:51:02.310 --> 00:51:02.360
Okay.

1324
00:51:02.630 --> 00:51:03.010
Anytime.

1325
00:51:03.310 --> 00:51:07.070
As you were talking, I had like eight questions about different episodes down the road.

1326
00:51:07.330 --> 00:51:08.110
I did, too.

1327
00:51:08.110 --> 00:51:10.270
So we will definitely be calling you back, Phil.

1328
00:51:10.450 --> 00:51:11.550
Thank you so much.

1329
00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:12.870
We miss you.

1330
00:51:13.010 --> 00:51:13.950
Yeah, let's keep Dunder Mifflin going.

1331
00:51:14.210 --> 00:51:15.270
I miss you as well.

1332
00:51:15.490 --> 00:51:18.170
Let's keep Dunder Mifflin relevant for the next 30 years.

1333
00:51:18.310 --> 00:51:18.870
I love that.

1334
00:51:18.990 --> 00:51:19.790
All right, Phil.

1335
00:51:19.810 --> 00:51:21.510
Well, have a good day.

1336
00:51:21.910 --> 00:51:23.590
Yeah, tell everyone we say hi over there.

1337
00:51:23.590 --> 00:51:23.970
Okay.

1338
00:51:24.670 --> 00:51:25.070
I will.

1339
00:51:25.270 --> 00:51:25.550
Thanks, Jenna.

1340
00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:26.030
Thanks, Angela.

1341
00:51:26.150 --> 00:51:26.310
All right.

1342
00:51:26.370 --> 00:51:26.670
Talk to you soon.

1343
00:51:26.690 --> 00:51:26.830
Bye.

1344
00:51:27.170 --> 00:51:27.290
Bye.

1345
00:51:27.510 --> 00:51:27.810
Thank you.

1346
00:51:27.950 --> 00:51:28.130
Bye.

1347
00:51:28.670 --> 00:51:29.650
What's next, Jenna?

1348
00:51:29.810 --> 00:51:31.270
I have three note cards left.

1349
00:51:31.470 --> 00:51:33.470
Well, now we need to talk about the cake.

1350
00:51:33.770 --> 00:51:34.710
Oh, yes.

1351
00:51:34.910 --> 00:51:38.150
The ice cream, the mint chocolate chip ice cream cake.

1352
00:51:38.390 --> 00:51:38.790
Yes.

1353
00:51:38.990 --> 00:51:41.210
I got so sick eating that cake.

1354
00:51:41.370 --> 00:51:46.370
This is the second most frequently asked question was about the cake and what the cake was like,

1355
00:51:46.370 --> 00:51:47.690
and did we really eat the cake?

1356
00:51:47.890 --> 00:51:49.630
Oh, we ate that cake, you guys.

1357
00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:50.290
And guess what?

1358
00:51:50.390 --> 00:51:51.190
You start filming.

1359
00:51:51.190 --> 00:51:54.210
We would start filming at 7.30 a.m.

1360
00:51:54.350 --> 00:51:54.490
Yeah.

1361
00:51:54.490 --> 00:51:57.830
So at 7.30 a.m., you're going to eat ice cream cake?

1362
00:51:57.850 --> 00:52:00.690
Someone hands you a plate of ice cream cake at 7.30 a.m.

1363
00:52:00.690 --> 00:52:02.570
And you're going to eat it for five hours?

1364
00:52:02.570 --> 00:52:03.210
All day.

1365
00:52:03.410 --> 00:52:03.610
All day.

1366
00:52:03.610 --> 00:52:05.630
And then they're like, let's break for lunch.

1367
00:52:05.810 --> 00:52:07.250
And you're like, I want to puke.

1368
00:52:07.430 --> 00:52:07.650
Yeah.

1369
00:52:07.870 --> 00:52:09.690
Let me say something about prop food.

1370
00:52:10.170 --> 00:52:10.970
Oh, do it.

1371
00:52:11.070 --> 00:52:12.750
BJ Novak loves prop food.

1372
00:52:12.890 --> 00:52:14.590
Mindy Kaling loves prop food.

1373
00:52:14.710 --> 00:52:16.070
I also kind of like it.

1374
00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:18.370
I get real excited when I have to snack in a scene.

1375
00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:20.830
I do not, because I know it's going to turn on me.

1376
00:52:20.930 --> 00:52:21.290
I know.

1377
00:52:21.350 --> 00:52:23.270
And it turns on me every time.

1378
00:52:23.270 --> 00:52:23.770
Every time.

1379
00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:25.330
And I still make the same mistakes.

1380
00:52:25.570 --> 00:52:25.950
Every time.

1381
00:52:26.050 --> 00:52:30.350
I would see Ed Helms, Mindy Kaling, you, BJ, be like, oh, prop food, and get so excited.

1382
00:52:30.350 --> 00:52:30.510
Yeah.

1383
00:52:30.590 --> 00:52:32.050
Like, guys, pace yourself.

1384
00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:32.310
I know.

1385
00:52:32.310 --> 00:52:33.050
It's going to get gross.

1386
00:52:33.290 --> 00:52:37.690
We'd have a party in the conference room, and they'd have, like, pigs in a blanket.

1387
00:52:38.290 --> 00:52:40.590
And you just, me and BJ would be over there.

1388
00:52:40.730 --> 00:52:42.390
I'd be like, guys, slow down.

1389
00:52:42.710 --> 00:52:42.850
I know.

1390
00:52:42.850 --> 00:52:44.950
You're going to have to eat that for four hours.

1391
00:52:45.230 --> 00:52:45.510
I know.

1392
00:52:45.590 --> 00:52:48.410
Well, this was the first time that I made the mistake.

1393
00:52:48.610 --> 00:52:51.890
And I started wolfing down ice cream cake early.

1394
00:52:51.890 --> 00:52:54.030
And then you have to match that in every shot.

1395
00:52:54.150 --> 00:52:57.610
So whatever you do in that first shot, you then have to match it.

1396
00:52:57.730 --> 00:53:00.250
So continuity-wise, it all makes sense.

1397
00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:01.050
And here's the thing.

1398
00:53:01.130 --> 00:53:04.830
I'm in the background of most of the ice cream eating scenes.

1399
00:53:04.990 --> 00:53:05.570
What are you thinking?

1400
00:53:05.570 --> 00:53:08.270
So I was wolfing it down in the background.

1401
00:53:08.270 --> 00:53:09.330
She didn't have to.

1402
00:53:09.490 --> 00:53:10.430
She was in the background.

1403
00:53:10.430 --> 00:53:15.710
I was in the background, and then by the time it was time for my close-up, I was so

1404
00:53:15.710 --> 00:53:16.330
sick.

1405
00:53:16.710 --> 00:53:20.110
I didn't want to eat the ice cream cake, but then I had to.

1406
00:53:20.610 --> 00:53:21.510
I'll tell you this.

1407
00:53:21.610 --> 00:53:27.270
I had a slice, and Michael walks up to Phyllis and I, and when he walks up, poor Steve is

1408
00:53:27.270 --> 00:53:29.770
like wolfing the cake down through him as he's talking.

1409
00:53:29.970 --> 00:53:32.630
He would eat a full piece every take.

1410
00:53:32.670 --> 00:53:33.110
Oh, man.

1411
00:53:33.230 --> 00:53:35.350
They eventually got a bucket, though, that he could spit.

1412
00:53:35.350 --> 00:53:36.070
That we could spit in.

1413
00:53:36.370 --> 00:53:37.670
Which is also disgusting.

1414
00:53:37.750 --> 00:53:38.570
It's called a spit bucket.

1415
00:53:38.570 --> 00:53:39.530
It's called a spit bucket.

1416
00:53:39.530 --> 00:53:44.370
But he would walk up eating the cake, and he was talking to Phyllis and Meredith and my

1417
00:53:44.370 --> 00:53:44.730
character.

1418
00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:48.130
And I was just going to take one bite as he walked up.

1419
00:53:48.510 --> 00:53:53.810
And by the end of the five hours of doing the scene, when I would scoop up that one bite,

1420
00:53:53.930 --> 00:53:57.410
my hand would start to shake because I was anticipating it.

1421
00:53:57.450 --> 00:53:58.210
So I was like, oh.

1422
00:53:59.390 --> 00:54:01.370
Yes, your body was rejecting it.

1423
00:54:01.370 --> 00:54:03.170
My body was rejecting it.

1424
00:54:03.290 --> 00:54:07.050
It was like, please don't put that mint chocolate chip ice cream in your mouth one more

1425
00:54:07.050 --> 00:54:08.650
time ever again for the rest of your life.

1426
00:54:08.790 --> 00:54:09.370
I can't eat it.

1427
00:54:09.370 --> 00:54:10.150
I can't eat it.

1428
00:54:10.310 --> 00:54:11.510
I can't eat it now.

1429
00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:13.370
My daughter loves mint chocolate chip.

1430
00:54:13.490 --> 00:54:14.510
I don't want it near me.

1431
00:54:14.650 --> 00:54:17.210
I loved ice cream cake in general.

1432
00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:17.650
No.

1433
00:54:17.850 --> 00:54:24.690
And only, it was last year for my son's seventh birthday, I finally ate a piece of ice cream

1434
00:54:24.690 --> 00:54:25.390
cake again.

1435
00:54:25.390 --> 00:54:29.710
But that's, it had been like, I'd gone on like a 15-year moratorium.

1436
00:54:29.990 --> 00:54:32.890
So the final thing I think we should talk about with this episode.

1437
00:54:33.110 --> 00:54:33.510
Oh, yeah, let's go on that note.

1438
00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:39.050
Is the scene with Roy coming into the office and confronting Jim.

1439
00:54:39.050 --> 00:54:40.790
You guys are so busted.

1440
00:54:41.250 --> 00:54:43.750
I remember like being over in accounting, being like, oh.

1441
00:54:43.870 --> 00:54:47.230
And also, David Dinman can be scary when he wants to be.

1442
00:54:47.230 --> 00:54:48.050
He can be intimidating.

1443
00:54:48.450 --> 00:54:49.830
He's such a teddy bear in real life.

1444
00:54:49.870 --> 00:54:50.290
I know.

1445
00:54:50.390 --> 00:54:51.330
He's so snuggly.

1446
00:54:51.450 --> 00:54:52.890
When he came in like that, I was like.

1447
00:54:53.050 --> 00:54:53.870
He's a big guy.

1448
00:54:53.870 --> 00:54:56.870
And I think he turns my character on because he's such a man.

1449
00:54:57.190 --> 00:54:59.330
Like you see that play out later where I'm like.

1450
00:54:59.650 --> 00:55:02.370
I'm clearly like enamored with Roy.

1451
00:55:02.550 --> 00:55:05.530
But in that moment, I was like, oh my God, Roy is going to kick Jim's ass.

1452
00:55:05.630 --> 00:55:05.790
Yeah.

1453
00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:08.350
Your character, Angela, likes a beefy alpha male.

1454
00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:09.250
For sure.

1455
00:55:09.490 --> 00:55:09.930
She just does.

1456
00:55:10.210 --> 00:55:10.830
That's true.

1457
00:55:10.990 --> 00:55:12.330
So I remember this scene.

1458
00:55:12.470 --> 00:55:13.650
I remember breaking it down.

1459
00:55:13.850 --> 00:55:21.450
We shot a ton of versions of this scene because we really didn't know how big of a reaction

1460
00:55:21.450 --> 00:55:22.270
Roy should have.

1461
00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:28.150
So the first thing I remember was that somehow in the scene when Jim and I are giggling about

1462
00:55:28.150 --> 00:55:31.610
our accomplishment, we had to find a way to hold hands.

1463
00:55:31.830 --> 00:55:33.550
That there had to be like.

1464
00:55:33.830 --> 00:55:34.390
An organic way.

1465
00:55:34.490 --> 00:55:36.350
An organic way where his arm is around me.

1466
00:55:36.430 --> 00:55:37.490
There had to be something.

1467
00:55:37.750 --> 00:55:43.090
Because Roy has walked in the door before and seen Jim standing at reception talking to me.

1468
00:55:43.090 --> 00:55:48.850
So we had to come up with this reason that John would come around the front of the desk.

1469
00:55:49.030 --> 00:55:49.770
He's so excited.

1470
00:55:49.990 --> 00:55:50.110
Yeah.

1471
00:55:50.190 --> 00:55:52.030
He's so excited that he jogs around.

1472
00:55:52.330 --> 00:55:56.370
So instead of coming up to me with the desk between us, which is what you've seen in the

1473
00:55:56.370 --> 00:55:58.130
past, this was a big moment.

1474
00:55:58.310 --> 00:56:00.470
He breaks that barrier.

1475
00:56:00.590 --> 00:56:01.730
He comes around the desk.

1476
00:56:01.810 --> 00:56:03.110
He puts his arm on me.

1477
00:56:03.110 --> 00:56:05.770
We end up kind of holding hands and giggling.

1478
00:56:06.050 --> 00:56:11.730
And I remember us really struggling to make sure that it looked real and organic and not

1479
00:56:11.730 --> 00:56:12.030
staged.

1480
00:56:12.150 --> 00:56:18.330
We didn't want to do something so out of the ordinary that you would be expecting something

1481
00:56:18.330 --> 00:56:18.890
to happen.

1482
00:56:19.070 --> 00:56:19.250
Right.

1483
00:56:19.630 --> 00:56:22.170
And I think we accomplished it.

1484
00:56:22.270 --> 00:56:26.830
And then when he burst in the door, there were a couple of takes where he pushed Jim up against

1485
00:56:26.830 --> 00:56:30.630
a wall where he got really more physical with Jim.

1486
00:56:30.910 --> 00:56:35.910
And so we shot all these different versions so that we could get it just right.

1487
00:56:36.130 --> 00:56:36.330
Right.

1488
00:56:36.530 --> 00:56:38.650
And the version they went with is the beSt.

1489
00:56:38.730 --> 00:56:42.370
I think if Roy had gotten too physical there, it just would have taken us all out of it because

1490
00:56:42.370 --> 00:56:43.010
we'd be like, whoa.

1491
00:56:43.210 --> 00:56:46.170
But I think just how ticked off he was.

1492
00:56:46.270 --> 00:56:49.190
And then I thought John was brilliant in that moment.

1493
00:56:49.250 --> 00:56:54.530
He was so believable when he was like, I don't even know how to explain this because he realizes

1494
00:56:54.530 --> 00:56:57.370
how ridiculous their whole day has been.

1495
00:56:57.570 --> 00:56:57.690
Yeah.

1496
00:56:57.870 --> 00:56:59.910
And how do you explain what they've been doing?

1497
00:57:00.270 --> 00:57:00.470
Yeah.

1498
00:57:01.410 --> 00:57:03.290
And they're so busted, though.

1499
00:57:03.610 --> 00:57:07.210
Like, it is a moment that made me totally cringe.

1500
00:57:07.810 --> 00:57:08.090
Yeah.

1501
00:57:08.250 --> 00:57:11.290
And you know that Pam and Roy had words about that.

1502
00:57:11.370 --> 00:57:12.410
Oh, the car ride home.

1503
00:57:12.570 --> 00:57:12.890
In the car.

1504
00:57:13.110 --> 00:57:13.450
Yes.

1505
00:57:13.670 --> 00:57:13.910
Yes.

1506
00:57:13.910 --> 00:57:14.610
Car ride home.

1507
00:57:14.730 --> 00:57:15.610
That was a tough one.

1508
00:57:15.710 --> 00:57:16.990
My husband and I always talk about that.

1509
00:57:17.090 --> 00:57:20.930
Like, if we'll be like at a dinner party or something and you see like a couple have like

1510
00:57:20.930 --> 00:57:24.510
an awkward exchange, I'll like lean over to my husband and be like, oof, car ride

1511
00:57:24.530 --> 00:57:24.790
home.

1512
00:57:25.170 --> 00:57:25.830
Car ride home.

1513
00:57:26.570 --> 00:57:28.550
That's going to be a rough one.

1514
00:57:29.070 --> 00:57:29.990
So that's it.

1515
00:57:30.090 --> 00:57:30.790
That's the alliance.

1516
00:57:31.090 --> 00:57:32.330
Can I say one more thing?

1517
00:57:32.510 --> 00:57:33.670
I want to say one more thing.

1518
00:57:33.850 --> 00:57:37.970
This episode has a special place in my family's life.

1519
00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:38.610
Why?

1520
00:57:38.930 --> 00:57:44.230
Because while we were shooting this episode, they got me out early so that I could go home

1521
00:57:44.230 --> 00:57:45.470
for my sister's wedding.

1522
00:57:45.670 --> 00:57:46.790
My real sister's wedding.

1523
00:57:46.830 --> 00:57:47.570
I had to travel.

1524
00:57:47.750 --> 00:57:51.170
So she got married while we were shooting this episode.

1525
00:57:51.170 --> 00:57:54.790
And this year, she celebrated her 15th wedding anniversary.

1526
00:57:55.270 --> 00:57:55.970
Isn't that sweet?

1527
00:57:56.070 --> 00:57:57.590
So when I was watching this, I remembered that.

1528
00:57:57.750 --> 00:57:59.870
That's like a fun full circle life moment, too.

1529
00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:00.330
I know.

1530
00:58:00.610 --> 00:58:04.390
Well, this is the thing you guys are going to hear a lot as we do this podcast is, you

1531
00:58:04.390 --> 00:58:07.390
know, this show spanned 10 years of our lives.

1532
00:58:07.550 --> 00:58:09.570
And so much life happens in those 10 years.

1533
00:58:09.670 --> 00:58:15.370
And we really do have all these special memories of our own lives mixed in with what was happening

1534
00:58:15.370 --> 00:58:15.930
on The Office.

1535
